body:
After working through some mobile networking trouble, we found that some cell providers work better with IPv6 while some work better with IPv4. 

In testing, we found that IPv6 connect tokens only work properly when the Yojimbo client is initialized with an IPv6 address to bind to.

```
// Will not work with an IPv6 address as the only server address in the connect token.
Client* client = new Client(GetDefaultAllocator(), Address("0.0.0.0"), config, adapter, time);
vs:
// Works with an IPv6 address in the connect token.
Client* client = new Client(GetDefaultAllocator(), Address("0:0:0:0:0:0:0:0"), config, adapter, time);
```

We then tracked this down to `netcode_socket_create` in netcode.c, the address the client binds to must match the protocol (IPv4 vs IPv6) of the address container in the connect token. While the address that's used for `sendto` is being handled as IPv6 correctly, it fails if the socket wasn't created for an IPv6 client.

At the moment, we're unsure whether this is a bug, or by design. Should netcode/yojimbo be able to handle connect tokens with both IPv4 and IPv6? Is it possible we're doing something wrong here? Is this a bug with one of these two libraries?

If this isn't a supported feature, would you have any suggestions for this? If it is, and seems to be working as expected, is it possible we're not binding the client address correctly?

nodes.body:
Working through the problem we have found a workaround. For testing purposes, we have been able to allow both ipv6 & ipv4 addresses to pass through the created socket by setting the client's address to ipv6 (eg `Address("0:0:0:0:0:0:0:0")`) & removing replacing the assignment of the client address in `netcode_socket_create` with `socket_address.sin6_addr = in6addr_any;`. Changes:

```
...
    // Don't force ipv6 only.  Allow both type of connections.

    if ( address->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV6 )
    {
        int yes = 0;
        if ( setsockopt( s->handle, IPPROTO_IPV6, IPV6_V6ONLY, (char*)&yes, sizeof(yes) ) != 0 )
        {
            printf( "error: failed to allow other protocols over ipv6\n" );
            netcode_socket_destroy( s );
            return NETCODE_SOCKET_ERROR_SOCKOPT_IPV6_ONLY_FAILED;
        }
    }
...
    if ( address->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV6 )
    {
        struct sockaddr_in6 socket_address;
        memset( &socket_address, 0, sizeof( struct sockaddr_in6 ) );
        socket_address.sin6_family = AF_INET6;
        socket_address.sin6_addr = in6addr_any;
        socket_address.sin6_port = htons( address->port );

        if ( bind( s->handle, (struct sockaddr*) &socket_address, sizeof( socket_address ) ) < 0 )
        {
            printf( "error: failed to bind socket (ipv6)\n" );
            netcode_socket_destroy( s );
            return NETCODE_SOCKET_ERROR_BIND_IPV6_FAILED;
        }
    }
...
```

The workaround above obviously has security issues and we were also forced to temporarily remove the whitelist check. On the other hand, the result was that both client and server were able to send & receive both ipv4 & ipv6 data over the same socket. 

One issue did arise when the client with an ipv6 address connected to the server through an ipv4 address. In this case, the `netcode_address_equal` function would reject the `NETCODE_CONNECTION_CHALLENGE_PACKET` coming `from` and ipv6 address while the `&client->server_address` was an ipv4 address. The snippet below adjusting the type check in `netcode_address_equal` allowed us to work around this problem as well:

```
    if ( a->type != b->type )
    {
        char ipv6[NETCODE_MAX_ADDRESS_STRING_LENGTH];
        char ipv4[NETCODE_MAX_ADDRESS_STRING_LENGTH];

        if ( a->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV6 && b->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV4 )
        {
            netcode_address_to_string( a, ipv6 );
            netcode_address_to_string( b, ipv4 );
        }
        else if ( a->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV4 && b->type == NETCODE_ADDRESS_IPV6 )
        {
            netcode_address_to_string( a, ipv4 );
            netcode_address_to_string( b, ipv6 );
        }
        else
        {
            return 0;
        }

        size_t size = (size_t) (strlen(ipv4) - (floor( log10( b->port ) ) + 1) + 1);
        return memcmp( ipv4, ipv6 + 8, size );
    }
```

The final result is that a single client can reach the `CONNECTED` state over ipv4 or ipv6. We are still looking for viable, secure solutions. The questions mentioned in the previous comment still stand: Is a netcode client intended to be able to connect to both ipv4 & ipv6? Would you have any suggestions for this?
